# =============================================================================
#  ⚠️  FAKE CREDENTIALS - PLANTED DELIBERATELY  ⚠️
# =============================================================================
#
#  These are NOT real credentials. They were never valid, are not associated
#  with any account or service, and grant access to nothing.
#
#  They exist to satisfy the LSCS DevSecOps exam requirement:
#
#     "You must deliberately add a vulnerable package [...] OR deliberately
#      commit a 'fake' API key. Demonstrate in your documentation how your
#      pipeline successfully detects and flags this vulnerability!"
#
#  This file lives ONLY on the `demo/leaked-secret` branch and is never merged
#  into main. Its whole purpose is to make the `secret-scan` job go red.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  WHY THIS IS NOT AN AWS KEY
#
#  The first version of this demo planted a realistic AWS access key
#  (see aws-credentials.sample.env, kept alongside this file as a record).
#  It could not be pushed. GitHub Push Protection rejected it server-side with
#  GH013, before any workflow ran - and on a public repository that enforcement
#  cannot be switched off at the repository level. We verified that: the API
#  call to disable it succeeds and reads back "disabled", and the push is
#  rejected anyway.
#
#  So the planted secret was rebuilt to target a real and deliberate gap:
#
#    - GitHub push protection matches PROVIDER patterns - an AKIA... key, a
#      ghp_... token, a Stripe sk_live_... key. It is high-precision by design,
#      because a false positive blocks someone's push.
#    - Gitleaks ALSO carries a `generic-api-key` rule, which fires on any
#      high-entropy value assigned to a secret-shaped name.
#
#  The token below matches no vendor's format, so it pushes cleanly, and
#  Gitleaks still catches it. That is the point worth understanding: the two
#  scanners are not redundant, and the one that runs earlier is deliberately
#  the narrower of the two.
#
#  Deliberately NOT imitating a vendor prefix either (no `sk_live_`-alike).
#  Faking a format we don't use would be a worse demo AND would risk tripping
#  the very control we are routing around, for no benefit.
#
#  Verified against the real scanner rather than assumed:
#    RuleID   generic-api-key
#    Entropy  4.66   (the rule requires > 3.5)
# =============================================================================

MACKY_INTERNAL_API_TOKEN=k4lcyeOHULESUGxBchUizB0hkb1l26cvOyvpvypeYQbd
MACKY_INTERNAL_API_BASE=https://internal.macky-merch.invalid/v1
